1
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:31,600
Hello everyone.

2
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:40,560
This afternoon, I will cover Session 2.

3
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:48,920
The topic is how to create test cases with GTest framework.

4
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:58,590
Has everyone installed the environment, and do you have any questions?

5
00:00:58,590 --> 00:01:24,690
If everything is ready, we will practice creating a basic test case for code coverage.

6
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:30,330
Today, I will cover four main parts.

7
00:01:30,330 --> 00:01:39,140
First, we will generate and build a GTest project.

8
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:51,830
Then, we will generate the TPA report, check code coverage, and perform a Delta review.

9
00:01:51,830 --> 00:01:59,090
Second, I will explain the test fixture used in my current project.

10
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:19,190
Third, we will apply the fixture to create test cases and discuss test data and techniques.

11
00:02:19,190 --> 00:02:39,020
Finally, I will demonstrate a sample GTest test case.

12
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:48,150
This morning, I showed you how to set up the environment.

13
00:02:48,150 --> 00:02:54,630
To test a C file, first make sure that the environment is working correctly.

14
00:02:54,630 --> 00:03:11,080
Drag the C file that you want to test onto the tool's .bat file.

15
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:35,700
A console will open, and the tool will analyze the file and copy the standard headers.

16
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:41,520
The generated project needs three main folders to work correctly.

17
00:03:41,520 --> 00:04:17,720
These folders contain the configuration, the module files, and the generated test project.

18
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:48,320
Before reviewing a project, delete the old Output folder to ensure that the result is clean.

19
00:04:48,320 --> 00:05:08,990
After generation, the console will display messages similar to those shown on the screen.

20
00:05:08,990 --> 00:05:49,070
The console can be hard to read, so pay close attention to the red and green messages.

21
00:05:49,070 --> 00:06:13,560
The tool also generates the files required to validate the module in the Cantata folder.

22
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:44,210
The validation package contains a validation file and a test report, so check the TPA file carefully.

23
00:06:44,210 --> 00:07:25,800
Next, use Cantata to check code coverage because the GTest tool cannot show it directly.

24
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:35,300
The Delta step is especially important.

25
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:44,730
In Session 1, I mentioned that GTest is useful for managing changes.

26
00:07:44,730 --> 00:07:55,430
Managing changes is simple when you review an existing test case or retest a module.

27
00:07:55,430 --> 00:08:06,000
For example, take the files that were already tested after a code update.

28
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:15,820
Put the .h and .cpp files into the new environment, then build the project again to obtain coverage.

29
00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:25,690
That completes the procedure for building a project with Cantata.

30
00:08:25,690 --> 00:08:34,850
Do you have any questions, or would a demonstration make this clearer?

31
00:08:34,850 --> 00:09:04,090
When I drag the C file onto Setup.bat, do I need to add the root-folder path?

32
00:09:05,860 --> 00:09:10,420
There are two ways to do this.

33
00:09:10,420 --> 00:09:21,340
For the current project, we do not need to build with the developer configuration, so we bypass that step.

34
00:09:21,340 --> 00:09:37,480
You can add the repository path so that the tool collects all configurations from the source into the project.

35
00:09:37,480 --> 00:09:51,510
However, that approach takes too long, so we use standard headers and copy them into our project.

36
00:09:51,510 --> 00:10:26,800
The Input folder contains the standard headers, and you can add the required parameters or variables there.

37
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:51,250
Can you demonstrate the entire process from the beginning?

38
00:10:51,250 --> 00:10:53,667
Yes.

39
00:10:53,667 --> 00:11:32,950
We need a C file and an environment that has already been set up.

40
00:11:32,950 --> 00:11:58,370
Drag the C file onto Setup.bat, and the tool will copy the headers into the Input folder before generating the project.

41
00:11:58,370 --> 00:12:31,550
If you include the repository, the tool can find missing definitions automatically; otherwise, you must define them yourself.

42
00:12:31,550 --> 00:12:48,280
Once the common definitions are already in place, later users usually have much less work to do.

43
00:12:48,280 --> 00:13:02,460
At that point, you only need to stub the remaining functions to test the module independently.

44
00:13:02,460 --> 00:13:07,550
Okay.

45
00:13:07,550 --> 00:13:27,750
Do you have any remaining questions about the procedure for building a Cantata project?

46
00:13:27,750 --> 00:13:35,200
After opening the project in VS Code, it is already built, so we just need to press Run, correct?

47
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:45,080
Yes.

48
00:13:45,080 --> 00:14:28,490
For C++ files, test cases still need to be written manually, but a later session covers test-case automation.

49
00:14:28,490 --> 00:15:30,180
Next, I will explain what a test fixture is.

50
00:15:30,180 --> 00:15:55,190
A test fixture helps us create multiple test cases in the same test file.

51
00:15:55,190 --> 00:16:47,790
It lets related test cases reuse common properties, which saves effort when a test file is large.

52
00:16:47,790 --> 00:17:10,040
I will demonstrate this fixture in tomorrow's session.

53
00:17:10,040 --> 00:17:21,080
Using a fixture also makes it much easier to review test cases written by someone else.

54
00:17:21,080 --> 00:17:39,580
Please refer to the documentation for TEST_F and TEST_P to understand these test forms more clearly.

55
00:17:39,580 --> 00:18:00,540
TEST_P is useful when you need to run the same test structure with many values of a parameter.

56
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:42,850
This may be difficult to understand at first, but the fixture creates multiple test cases from one template.

57
00:18:42,850 --> 00:19:35,610
When creating a fixture, choose a test name and define the input, output, and expected-result structure.

58
00:19:35,610 --> 00:20:17,710
Do you have any questions about the test fixture before we move on?

59
00:20:17,710 --> 00:21:06,820
I divide test-case creation into four main parts, beginning with the purpose of the test.

60
00:21:06,820 --> 00:21:20,370
In the test-case definition, first declare the variables that you need for testing.

61
00:21:20,370 --> 00:21:28,660
Next, set the required values for the variables you have defined.

62
00:21:28,660 --> 00:21:45,900
Then, call the function under test.

63
00:21:45,900 --> 00:22:02,920
The values that you set are the inputs passed to that function.

64
00:22:02,920 --> 00:22:22,460
After the function runs through its logic, check whether the expected values have changed as intended.

65
00:22:22,460 --> 00:22:57,510
This is only a template adapted from Cantata, so define the structure in the way that best fits your project.

66
00:23:04,320 --> 00:23:07,300
Is this part clear to everyone?

67
00:23:15,610 --> 00:23:25,370
What is a stub function, and how is it different from the function under test?

68
00:23:27,800 --> 00:23:34,700
Thank you for the question.

69
00:23:34,700 --> 00:24:28,220
Unit testing isolates the source code that you want to test from the rest of the repository.

70
00:24:28,220 --> 00:24:51,530
When code is isolated, definitions from other files can be missing.

71
00:24:51,530 --> 00:25:01,320
A stub defines those missing dependent functions, while the function under test is the function we actually want to verify.

72
00:25:01,320 --> 00:25:25,300
Call the function under test so that the input values are passed to it and its behavior can be checked.

73
00:25:25,300 --> 00:26:06,020
If there are no more questions, we will move on to the next topic.

74
00:26:06,020 --> 00:26:50,140
Here is an example of a normal test.

75
00:26:50,140 --> 00:27:48,110
I will introduce syntax for accessing static-function and local-static variables during testing.

76
00:27:48,110 --> 00:28:12,820
These variables are declared inside the source file and cannot be controlled directly in the normal way.

77
00:28:12,820 --> 00:28:32,080
The provided syntax lets us access the variable's memory location and change its value for testing.

78
00:28:32,080 --> 00:29:15,160
For a global static variable, write the module name and then the name of the global variable.

79
00:29:15,160 --> 00:29:34,900
Yes, this syntax is used in the C++ test file, and it must follow the exact format.

80
00:29:34,900 --> 00:29:58,110
Please save this syntax and use it exactly when you need it.

81
00:29:58,110 --> 00:30:09,080
Can you explain the scope class name in the static-variable syntax?

82
00:30:09,080 --> 00:30:21,480
The previous form supports local static variables, while the other form supports global static variables in C files.

83
00:30:21,480 --> 00:30:31,190
A local static variable is a static variable declared inside a function.

84
00:30:31,190 --> 00:30:52,800
I will demonstrate this syntax later so that it is easier to understand.

85
00:30:52,800 --> 00:31:20,730
Next, I will explain how to create a stub function.

86
00:31:20,730 --> 00:32:29,950
Suppose a source file uses a variable or function that is not defined in the generated project.

87
00:32:29,950 --> 00:32:36,080
To use it in the C++ test file, follow three steps.

88
00:32:36,080 --> 00:32:58,120
First, use a GMock method and then define the behavior of the function.

89
00:32:58,120 --> 00:33:31,850
Copy the function signature so that the test file knows the function name, return type, and parameters.

90
00:33:31,850 --> 00:33:55,390
Next, create the GMock method by defining the function name, type, and arguments.

91
00:33:55,390 --> 00:34:41,820
Then, specify what the mock function should do when it receives particular inputs.

92
00:34:41,820 --> 00:35:04,190
For example, you can return the same value that was passed in, or return a value that you specify.

93
00:35:04,190 --> 00:35:14,820
Do you have any questions about defining a stub in the test script?

94
00:35:14,820 --> 00:35:52,930
How can we change the output value of a stub function when different values are required?

95
00:35:52,930 --> 00:35:56,640
Thank you for the question.

96
00:35:56,640 --> 00:36:06,860
The setup has three parts: the function name, the mock method, and the defined behavior.

97
00:36:06,860 --> 00:37:04,300
In the test case, configure the return value that the stub function should provide for the required call.

98
00:37:04,300 --> 00:37:16,150
Use WillOnce when the function is called only once.

99
00:37:16,150 --> 00:37:49,380
Use WillRepeatedly when the same function is called many times and should return the required value repeatedly.

100
00:37:49,380 --> 00:38:44,980
This is the basic method for handling function behavior in the test script.

101
00:38:44,980 --> 00:39:08,730
You can also set a value for an argument when the argument holds an address.

102
00:39:08,730 --> 00:39:24,520
Because the argument is a pointer, you can write the required value directly to the memory location.

103
00:39:24,520 --> 00:40:05,790
Specify the argument index, starting from zero, and then set the value that you want to pass.

104
00:40:05,790 --> 00:41:32,820
The Return action specifies the value returned by the function.

105
00:41:32,820 --> 00:43:14,100
You can also pass a matching variable through the pointer, including a global variable when appropriate.

106
00:43:14,100 --> 00:43:50,010
That completes the concepts for this section, so we can move on to the example.

107
00:43:50,010 --> 00:44:36,660
The example shows how to set a local static variable.

108
00:44:36,660 --> 00:45:14,010
There are two possible methods, but the second method is generally recommended.

109
00:45:14,010 --> 00:45:40,920
Avoid overlapping values, because the tool may report the value as undefined when values conflict in the same memory location.

110
00:45:40,920 --> 00:45:57,790
Following the code flow can avoid this issue, although it may not give you complete control of the value.

111
00:45:57,790 --> 00:47:06,330
Let us check the syntax for accessing a local static variable.

112
00:47:06,330 --> 00:47:18,040
Use the file name, function name, and local static-variable name in the required syntax.

113
00:47:18,040 --> 00:47:39,220
A local static variable can be used only after the program reaches it and allocates its memory during the test run.

114
00:47:39,220 --> 00:47:46,748
Okay.

115
00:47:46,748 --> 00:49:09,560
Let us move to a sample so that the process is clearer.

116
00:49:09,560 --> 00:49:19,570
Using the concepts we have discussed, we will now create a basic test and check code coverage.

117
00:49:19,570 --> 00:49:31,400
Drag the C file onto the tool's .bat file, wait for the console, and press Enter.

118
00:49:31,400 --> 00:50:09,980
Open the generated project in VS Code; it includes standard headers, although some macros and definitions may still be missing.

119
00:50:09,980 --> 00:50:51,130
The generated template has two main files: a header file for missing definitions and a C++ test script.

120
00:50:51,130 --> 00:51:34,940
Use the header file to declare missing functions or variables from other files.

121
00:51:34,940 --> 00:52:11,460
Use the C++ file to define stubs and write the test script.

122
00:52:11,460 --> 00:52:39,450
The test fixture contains test suites, and each test suite can contain multiple test cases.

123
00:52:39,450 --> 00:53:47,140
Every test case must have a unique name, and you can name them after the function and a test number.

124
00:53:47,140 --> 00:55:08,690
Build the project from the Terminal and use the console output to find errors, such as undefined references.

125
00:55:11,160 --> 00:55:39,480
When an item is undefined, add the required definition to the test-suite file.

126
00:55:48,330 --> 00:56:30,800
For a function with multiple parameters, declare each parameter according to the type and behavior that you need.

127
00:56:30,800 --> 00:56:39,100
After resolving all errors, the console will display a result like this.

128
00:56:39,100 --> 00:56:49,300
This means that the configuration step is complete and you can begin test-case design.

129
00:56:49,300 --> 00:57:03,000
To design a test case, start by declaring the function from the C file in the test script.

130
00:57:03,000 --> 00:57:21,060
This tells the test script that the C file contains the function that you want to test.

131
00:57:21,060 --> 00:58:17,400
Now, write the test case in the same basic structure used by Cantata and other tools.

132
00:58:17,400 --> 01:00:20,870
Declare the input variables, call the function, and check the expected return value.

133
01:00:20,870 --> 01:00:59,850
After the test passes, run the Cantata script to generate the reports required for delivery.

134
01:00:59,850 --> 01:01:35,280
These include the test report and the TPA file.

135
01:01:35,280 --> 01:03:17,360
For a long source file, use Cantata++ coverage measurement to see which code has been covered.

136
01:03:17,360 --> 01:04:25,240
Create a Cantata C project, add the relevant C/C++ file, and open CodeCoveredViewer to see coverage in green and red.

137
01:04:25,240 --> 01:04:32,680
Do you have any questions about creating the Cantata project for the coverage viewer?

138
01:04:32,680 --> 01:05:23,780
Can the viewer filter the test cases when a source file has many functions?

139
01:05:23,780 --> 01:05:37,470
The viewer does not provide an automatic filter, but you can use a manual filter in the C++ test script.

140
01:05:37,470 --> 01:06:03,290
In the documentation, you can filter a single test case to show its corresponding coverage colors.

141
01:06:03,290 --> 01:06:19,520
What if the C file is very large and running every test case takes too long?

142
01:06:19,520 --> 01:06:36,860
Use the manual filter to include the test case that you need or exclude a test case you do not want to run.

143
01:06:36,860 --> 01:08:12,460
That completes the basic test demonstration.

144
01:08:12,460 --> 01:10:09,880
Next, we will look at examples involving local static and global static variables.

145
01:10:09,880 --> 01:11:05,030
For a global static variable, write its type and variable name in the specified syntax, then set the required value.

146
01:11:05,030 --> 01:11:29,710
VS Code debugging is useful here because you can set a breakpoint and press F5.

147
01:11:29,710 --> 01:12:51,650
The values defined in the test case are passed into the function, and you can inspect them as the code runs.

148
01:12:51,650 --> 01:14:26,000
For a local static variable, follow the exact convention: module name, function name, and local variable name.

149
01:14:26,000 --> 01:14:56,620
Do not access a local static variable in the first test case before the program has created its memory location.

150
01:14:56,620 --> 01:16:33,440
Before debugging, run the test first so that the debug run is based on the latest execution.

151
01:16:33,440 --> 01:16:43,570
In this example, the local static variable is set to 10 and passed to the function under test.

152
01:16:43,570 --> 01:16:59,050
When the value overlaps with another assignment, the variable can be reset during code execution.

153
01:16:59,050 --> 01:17:11,310
The tool may then report the local static variable as undefined because the required value was not retained.

154
01:17:11,310 --> 01:17:51,290
This is an important limitation when testing code that relies on local static variables.

155
01:17:51,290 --> 01:18:27,020
Should the coverage report describe this as a tool limitation when the corresponding code cannot be covered?

156
01:18:27,020 --> 01:18:27,508
No.

157
01:18:27,508 --> 01:18:41,980
Coverage cannot be claimed simply because a local static variable is difficult to access.

158
01:18:41,980 --> 01:18:56,920
If the code logic never produces the required value, the relevant branch cannot be covered.

159
01:18:56,920 --> 01:19:15,440
In that case, the issue comes from the code logic, not from a limitation of the tool.

160
01:19:15,440 --> 01:19:16,133
Okay.

161
01:19:16,133 --> 01:19:25,420
Please contact me afterward if you need to discuss a specific case.

162
01:19:26,640 --> 01:21:24,850
You need to practice this workflow to identify real issues and understand how to apply it.

163
01:21:24,850 --> 01:22:24,320
After today's training, I will send a task in the module; follow the example and email me if you have questions.

164
01:22:24,320 --> 01:22:41,730
If there are no more questions, we will end today's training.

165
01:22:41,730 --> 01:22:48,580
Thank you for attending.
